Module 7, lesson 6's hand-written approach (one if statement per rule) becomes unwieldy as rules multiply. A schema library (this lesson uses Zod as a representative example) lets an entire validation schema be declared once, directly extending Module 12's TypeScript type system into runtime-enforced validation:

import { z } from 'zod';

const createUserSchema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  password: z.string().min(8),
  age: z.number().int().positive().optional(),
  bio: z.string().max(500).optional()
});

// Validating - directly replacing Module 7's many individual if checks
function validateCreateUser(data: unknown) {
  const result = createUserSchema.safeParse(data);
  if (!result.success) {
    throw new ValidationError(result.error.issues.map((i) => i.message).join(', '));
  }
  return result.data; // genuinely typed AND validated, both at once
}

Sanitisation, as a genuinely separate, additional step:

import sanitizeHtml from 'sanitize-html';

function sanitizeComment(rawText: string): string {
  return sanitizeHtml(rawText, { allowedTags: [], allowedAttributes: {} });
  // strips ALL HTML tags, regardless of whether rawText already
  // passed length/format validation - a genuinely separate concern
}

